>Zane H. Healy wrote:

> In alt.sys.pdp11 Tim Shoppa <shoppa@trailing-edge.com> wrote:
> > Megan has asked here before, but I too would like to see other folks
> > make suggestions of what should be done in the next *major*
> > release, RT-11 6.0, since Mentec/DigiPaq is almost out of tenths :-).
> > Bring back the SJ monitor?  Support in the kernel for TCP/IP?  Unified
> > support throughout the OS and CUSPS for partitions larger than
> > 65 kBlocks?  Yep, look in your SSM's, there's been a word reserved
> > for the cluster factor on every pack, it's just never been used.
> > Now's the time to ask, everyone!
> Support for larger disks and partitions sound like a really good idea to me.
> While still possible to find drives under 2Gb, the only easy solution for
> getting NEW drives that size are removables such as Zip Drives.  This isn't
> a solution I like, as I don't like the reliablity of modern removables.  I
> kind of hate wasting large chunks of a drive because the OS can't access
> them.

Jerome Fine replies:

Just thought I should correct an impression that seems to be in error.  Since
V5.3 of RT-11 when MSCP partitions were introduced (up to V5.4G only
8 partitions were allowed - since V5.5, up to 64 partitions are allowed),
the maximum partition number has been 255 (decimal) or 377 octal.  The
mapping table is two words:  first word is the UNIT, next byte is the PARTition,
last byte is the PORT (controller) number.  256 partitions allows a hard
disk drive up to 8 GBytes, not just 2 GBytes.  Naturally, even V5.5, V5.6 and
V5.7 can't do all 256 partitions at the same time, just 64.

As a comment, I consider the use of a whole word for the UNIT in the mapping
table a waste since the PARTition could have been given a whole word and
the UNIT would have been very satisfied with just a byte.  But I suspect there
may be a few cases when controllers allow the unit offset to be 256 which would
need more than a byte to hold the UNIT value.

On the other hand, for those of us who don't need a UNIT greater than 255 (decimal)
and have a hard disk drive greater that 8 GBytes (hubba hubba hubba - can I get
one as well?), I suppose that the mapping table could swap the UNIT and PARTition.
NOTE:  doing so is not likely to be simple or straightforward in the MSCP device
driver and might require a large number of changes to the MSCP device driver!

With regard to partitions larger than 32 Mbytes (greater that 65536 blocks), I am
curious how the CSW (Channel Status Words) would handle that situation?
Since the starting block number is limited to 16 bits, how would the location
of the file be determined?  Tim, perhaps you might answer that question?
Would there need to be another table with the cluster size for each device?
The table could not be just for the MSCP device driver since there can
be many hard disk drives on each controller as well as many controllers.

There is also a word for the block number of the first directory segment on a
disk drive.  At the moment, that value is always 6 and many programs have
hard coded the 6 value into the instructions, so I suspect that changing the
value might be rather difficult, although I have added an option to DIR
to allow for just that situation ("/VOLUME:StartingBlockNumber") so that
the directory of a DSK can be obtained without having to MOUNT the file.

> Of course how well does interfacing the new SCSI Hard Drives with the old
> PDP-11 SCSI adaptors work?  I'm noticing that not only is it getting more
> difficult to find small SCSI drives, it's also getting difficult to find
> plain 50-pin drives.  Can you use a 68-pin UW SCSI drive with a adaptor?  Of
> course now even the UW SCSI drives are becoming less prevalent as the U2W
> drives find greater popularity, and the U3W drives are on the way.  But,
> none of this is an OS issue :^)

I am interested in this question as well.  I have also heard of some interest in
the development of an IDE controller for the Qbus after the patents on the
MSCP expire next year and DEC/Compaq are no longer able to demand
the troll's license fee that CMD and others paid for MSCP host adapters,
or at least that has been what I have seen in the discussions.

> The TCP/IP support would be nice from my point of view, but is it something
> that is really needed?  TCP/IP support can be added to RT-11 if needed.

Sounds like a low priority since TCP/IP is already available to RT-11.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine




