In alt.sys.pdp11 Peter da Silva <peter@abbnm.com> wrote:
> I've heard that rumor. My experience during the '80s was that UNIX systems
> were more compatible with each other than different compilers for the same
> hardware and OS in the PC world. Wizard C versus Lattice C versus Aztec C,
> at least 2 versions of Modula-2, Turbo Pascal versus UCSD Pascal, etc...
> I never had that sort of problem with FORTRAN.
>
> The whole industry was fracturing into many incompatible components, UNIX
> held together better than most.

  A big part of the problem was that AT&T/Western Electric demanded that
commercial [redistribution] licensees *not* call their product "Unix". This
caused a lot of <mumble>ix variants (and the occasional <mumble>yx), all of
which were based on a nearly-identical code base (in many cases, exactly
identical except for different machdep code and device drivers).

  I once worked at Lifeboat Associates, where one of our projects was to
take PDP-11 Unix and change all the references in all the files and doc-
umentation to read "Xenix" and then kit the whole thing up and send it back
to Microsoft.

