>Megan Gentry wrote:

> Michael M. Iloff <dejamail@mosesele.de> writes:
>
> >V5.5 has been patched for Y2K indeed. Since 1997, the URL
> >"http://www.mosesele.de/year2k.htm" has been waiting for resonance.
>
> I would suspect that with such a URL, they are not *official*
> patches.  There were no official patches to RT V5.5 regarding
> Y2k either by Digital or Mentec.

Jerome Fine replies:

Of course these patches are not official - the ONLY official patches
are the ones provided by Mentec (most of which were probably
obtained free or nearly so since even Mentec acknowledges
that much of the effort was "contributed") at a cost of slightly over
$ US 1600 (which includes the DOCS for V5.6 of RT-11).
Note that this price is about the same as DEC charged for V5.6
of RT-11 back in 1992, but the percentage of new content is
substantially less - most of the differences are for bug fixes for
either Y2K compliance (which DEC does not admit was a bug)
or for other things which did not function or work correctly in
V5.6 of RT-11.

I note that I was immediately jumped on last time I mentioned that
I had Y2Y patches available for much of V5.4G of RT-11 when
I was unable to include a price for what was available due to a
lack of knowledge of just how much interest there was.  Naturally,
the Y2K patches for V5.4G of RT-11 are also unofficial and
just as obviously, Mentec will not support any user who avails
themselves of unofficial Y2K patched versions for V5.4G of RT-11.

On the other hand, Stuart Brook from Mentec has previously stated
that Mentec no longer supports any previous versions of RT-11 in
any case (V5.6 was supported for 6 months after the release of V5.7),
so I don't see what difference there is in using an unsupported version
of RT-11 such as V5.5 which was obtained directly from DEC or
a V5.5 with Y2K patches.  Except of course that the Y2K patches
for V5.5 of RT-11 are likely to be less expensive that a full V5.7
from Mentec.  Incidentally, Moses, do you have a price for the Y2K
patches for V5.5 of RT-11?  At this point, if there was sufficient
interest for Y2K patches for V5.4G of RT-11 (probably at least
10 good people), the price would be about $ US 300 per set to
be used on each CPU.

> Therefore, use of such patches probably would preclude your
> getting support from Mentec if something goes wrong.  You would
> have to demonstrate that the problem occurred with the unpatched
> system.  If someone is running this system (and applications)
> commercially, then this may not be a viable route.

In this statement, Megan Gentry is probably quite correct.  Any
company who has sufficient funds to upgrade their applications
would likely find the the purchase of V5.7 or RT-11 is such a
minor additional expenditure that the payment of any money
at all for a set of Y2K patches means that V5.7 is the ONLY
realistic alternative.  Why bother spending $ US 300 for Y2K
patches for V5.4G when V5.7 is only $ US 1600.  Plus, V5.7
has one major enhancement over V5.4G, an extended MSCP
device driver which allows a hard disk drive (or a group per
controller or host adapter - note SCSI host adapters are still
reasonably expensive) of up to 2 GBytes to be accessed
using all 64 partitions simultaneously.  On the other hand, I
have also looked at producing a set of commercial Y2K patches
for V5.6 of RT-11 which would likely be about the same price
as the Y2K set of patches for V5.4G of RT-11.  Plus, some
goodies thrown in like an MSCP device driver which is able to
do a software boot for all 64 partitions of DU(X).SYS rather
than just the first 8 partitions.  But I don't have the time right now
to help just one user.  Probably late this year.

> Also, it is not known here (though I hope it is spelled out on
> the web page) exactly what was fixed for Y2K and what might not
> have been...

Actually, there was not all that much to fix.  Whoever did V5.7
was working from the sources and therefore, it was MUCH easier.
I suspect that patching V5.5 was a much more difficult job.

Incidentally, I had aimed to put out a set of hobby patches for
V5.3 or RT-11 by the end of the summer, but as usual I was
delayed.  Plus, the last time I mentioned the possibility, there
was virtually no interest - certainly not even 10 good people.
Since V5.3 or RT-11 is available for hobby users who wish
to run V5.3 of RT-11 under the Supnik emulator (of which
I have a copy that runs on a PC under W95/W98), I do not
see anything to prevent others from doing the same with the
Y2K patches for V5.3 of RT-11.  ASAP I will try and complete
the Y2K patches for at least DIR.  Since any user can at least
use ODT to set the DATE value, that would at least be a start
and would also indicate the level of interest.  If those 10 good
people want to start coming out of the woodwork, please send
me an e-mail.  Naturally, Mentec supports neither the official
V5.3 of RT-11 at this point NOR the Y2K patched V5.3 of
RT-11.  Note that there will be a nominal gift for the hobby
version of the Y2K patches of V5.3 of RT-11 since that seems
to be the only way to determine the users who are truly interested,
somewhere between $ US 1 and $ US 10.  Most hobby users
seem determined to obtain all of their software at zero cost.  Even
$ US 1 seems to turn off anyone who just wants to find out if it
is possible to just have the newer version.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine
RT-11/TSX-PLUS  User/Addict




