Jerome Fine replies:

From what I can understand, the only difference between a TK50
media and the TK70 media is that the label on the first is brown
and says CompactTape while the label on the second is in blue
and says CompactTape II.  I presume that the TK70 knows that
the blue label is OK to use in the TK70 drive.

More specifically, once a blank tape has been written in a TK50,
the TK70 drive can recognize that the tape has been in the TK50
and will immediately WRITE  PROTECT  the CompactTape (II)
and the TK70 drive will not write on the tape.  On the other
hand, a friend gave me some TK50 tapes that were completely
blank (never written on before) and I was able to put them in
a TK70 and use them successfully.

I haven't tried the reverse, but I am told that a TK70 tape
which is placed in a TK50 drive can be used (initialized
and everything else) in the TK50 drive, but don't try
and use it in the TK70 drive thereafter and expect to
write the tape in the TK70 drive since the TK70 will
thereafter think it is a TK50 tape.

I have been told that a hand held degausser (looks like
an iron and is about half the size) can erase TK50 tapes
and allow a TK70 tape drive to use them successfully
in the TK70 drive.  As soon as I have achieved that,
I will have a number of TK70 tapes.

I don't know about the CompactTape III (DLT tapes)
tapes at this point, but I can let you know when I have
an opportunity to test one.

In the US and Canada, Radio Shack is supposed to sell
a hand held degausser which can erase tapes.  Other
stores may carry them in the UK.  If you are unable to
find any TK70 tapes (or for that matter TK50 tapes),
I have a few spare ones at this point and as soon as I
can verify that passing a TK50 tape through the degausser
works, I would agree to sell you some.  But the shipping
across the pond may be too high.  If you want, find out
what the shipping would be from Toronto for a package
of ten tapes.

