     One virtue of "non-public" sources is that "official" releases are
identified, presumably "certified" (meaning someone has taken the
responsibility for checking them out), and there
is support.

     Recall what happened to u**x in the 80's.  It fragmented into "many
incompatible components, unable to communicate with each other" (analogous to
"twisty little passages").
Paradoxically, Linux (which is, after all, "public" and "user-supported") may
be reversing
the trend.  However, the size of the Linux core community, and the presence of
commercial enterprises like Red Hat to ensure/enforce some standards, is much
greater than one might find for an RT-11 user community.

     On the other hand, if official development and support of RT-11 were ever
to be discontinued completely, it would be nice to release the sources ...

