> a solution I like, as I don't like the reliablity of modern removables.  I
> kind of hate wasting large chunks of a drive because the OS can't access
> them.

RT-11 5.5 and later do access up to 64 partitions of 32 Mb each,
for 2 Gb total.  Updating RT-11 to allow for larger partitions via
cluster sizes is certainly no small feat - all the monitors will
have to be extensively reworked for such support, as well as
many of the CUSPs that directly access the disk.  The existing
RT-11 DU drivers certainly allow for easy access to drives of up to
2**32 blocks (2.2 Terabytes, by my calculations) by physical
block numbers.

> Of course how well does interfacing the new SCSI Hard Drives with the old
> PDP-11 SCSI adaptors work?  I'm noticing that not only is it getting more
> difficult to find small SCSI drives, it's also getting difficult to find
> plain 50-pin drives.  Can you use a 68-pin UW SCSI drive with a adaptor?

Yes, you can.  Or you can use a SCA drive with a 50-pin adapter
on it.

> The TCP/IP support would be nice from my point of view, but is it something
> that is really needed?  TCP/IP support can be added to RT-11 if needed.

Indeed, Alan Baldwin's TCP/IP package works very nicely.  But
it's layered on top of, rather than an integral part of, RT-11.
I was thinking of TCP/IP support directly in the monitor, with
hooks for all the CUSP's to directly access information on remote
machines, support for incoming Telnet sessions without looping
back serial ports, etc.

Nice to see an actual constructive response to the question other
than the drones of "gimme gimme gimme for free" that have come
from other respondents!

