Bradford A. Lubell wrote:
>
> Does anybody have the binary distributions for RT-11 v5.5 or later.  I
> have a couple of systems that will be working after the end of the
> century and need some Y2K compatibility.  Posting and notes in places
> say that this was partially fixed in V5.5

Not in the 5.5 *code*, but in the 5.5 manuals you'll find the
"age bits" (which extend the RT-11 date word to the year 2099)
documented.  In the same manuals, you'll also see that DEC said
that there was no support inside RT-11 5.5 for the age bits.  And,
indeed, if you check the 5.5 sources there are lots of places where
it's noted that date input/output will fail horribly come 2000
(much less 2004, where the age bits actually kick in.)

> with better fixes in V5.6 and
> V5.7 (the last I believe).

Indeed, 5.7 is fully Y2K compliant, in the base operating
system, distributed utilities, and the system libraries.  A
couple of the distributed 5.6 utilities (notably MACRO.SAV and BUP.SAV)
are Y2K compliant, but the rest of the utilities and the 5.6 monitors
and the system libraries are not.  5.6 also doesn't support Y2K
on the 11/93 or Professional TOY clocks, while the 5.7 does support
these properly.  (You also need a firmware upgrade for the 11/93.)

So, why do you need the 5.5 binaries?  Sure, they'll fix many of
the bugs in early RT-11 5.x releases, but they won't do you any good
for Y2K compliance.  Sounds to me like you're
a prime candidate for upgrading to 5.7!  See http://www.mentec.com/

