As long as we are on the ancedote side: I know somebody who got a surplus
nine-track tape drive for home use. These things have a huge capacitor bank
to ride over the power supply while the motor starts up. After killing the
building's circuit breaker a number of times when powering the drive on, he
attached a one Ohm resistor (a ceramic body about the size of a largish piece
of bread) with a Reed relais to the incmong power line. That low-pass filtered
the capacitors's power draw to a tolerable value on power-on, but required the
resistor to be cooled - he put it into a pail of water. One day, the relais
didn't switch off as it should. He found the water had evaporated, and the
resistor had then desoldered itself from the rest of the circuit.

	Jan



