Peter da Silva <peter@abbnm.com> writes:
> Sure? We have some drives at work that claim to be RRD40s when queried by
> SHOW DEV, and they use regular CD caddies. Of course they could be lying.

  Yup. RRD40's use LMSI caddies, which are a bizarre sleeve (not flip-top)
surrounding some insect-mandible-like things that grasp the CD by the edge.
It is disturbingly similar to the CED videodisc caddy.

  RRD50's were table-top caddy-less boat-anchors, also made by LMSI (and
having the distinction of being slower than 1X, since the seek time could
be measured in fortnights.

  RRD42's were the start of the "normal" drives, being a Sony CDU-540 which
used the normal flip-top caddy.

