We have found in a review of a sampling of VAX and Alpha system users that
almost all companies with more than a few systems have lost track of a
substantial number of licenses from the date of original acquisition. In one
case we found unused (and unknown) licenses whose present market value is in
excess of one million dollars.

Most of these companies fail to realize that they have a huge asset that
they are not keeping track of and whose value jumps greatly each year. These
unused assets can be converted to trade-in credits when upgrading, buying
new software or new systems. Even for companies converting to new platforms,
much of the older software can be traded-in for use on the new platform.

Some companies sell off their old system with minimal or no software when
upgrading or converting. By doing so they receive only 30% to 60% of the
true asset value.

I can be reached at bobwalsh@ma.ultranet.com






