Of the top of my head a 11/730 is roughly 1/3 the speed of a 11/750.  Oops,
I just checked and I guess I was wrong, it's actually 1/2 the speed of a 
11/750, didn't realize a 11/750 is only 0.6 VUPS.

> One real sad computer collector I encountered wanted a 730
> because apparently its all TTL, no custom chips so you can fix it
> with a scope and a Maplins catalogue :-)

This sounds like an excellent reason.  On a simular note, I prefer RL02
drives and diskpacks to something such as a RD54, despite the fact the RD54
has about 15x the capacity.  I figure those RL02's will still be working
when the computer you buy new today has fallen apart and is in the trash
heap.

> Anyway, even a MicroVAX II is to slow and large for me to bother
> with.

Yes, BUT they make excellent chassis for Q-Bus PDP-11's (my main PDP-11
started life as a MicroVAX II, gotta love the BA123).  Except the processor
and RAM you can even leave most/all the other parts in place when you
upgrade it to a PDP-11.

Actually I think MicroVAX II's are good machines, and they do have their
uses, both in Hobbyist and Work environments.  The only real downside of a
MicroVAX II is the cost of adding decent Hard Drives.  Although replacing
the RQDX3 with a KDA50 or 3rd party ESDI controller is a good start.

