In article <37DDDB10.3D784F05@tsoft-inc.com>,
David A Froble  <davef@tsoft-inc.com> wrote:
>I have it on good authority that the VAX 11/730 was built solely for
>misdirection, thus sending the Russians in a bad direction, with the
>centerpoint of the ruse being that damn tape drive. :-)

Yeah.  Pity DEC throught the TU58 was a good idea for other systems as
well...

A standard joke in a former life was to secret a stack of TU58 carts in
software kit boxes, then pretend to open it and extract the TU58s as if
they were the distribution media instead of the expected 9track tape...

We also had an 11/750 in the depths Parliament Buildings that was often
unused, and therefore had some chance of writing a standalone BACKUP kit
onto TU58 without dying partway through due to missed interrupts (the
TU58 interface was simply a single-buffered UART; on a busy system it
could fail to extract characters from the UART in time, which would
stuff up the RSP protocol it used, and the recovery was non-existent).
Building three or four Standalone BACKUP kits for all our 750s was a
good excuse for disappearing for an afternoon if I didn't want to be
found...

-- don



