Paul DeMone <pdemone@igs.net> writes:

> Kevin Oberman wrote:
> > 
> > "Richard D. Piccard" <piccard@ohio.edu> writes:
> > 
>    My understanding of those days was it was red semi-transparent
>    film that was manually cut out.  The stuff was called ruby-lith or
>    something like that. *That* was before my time but I saw some of
>    this stuff old-timers at Nortel kept around.
> 
>    I do remember tapeouts in GDSII on 1/2 magnetic tape reels.  This
>    tape was read onto a mainframe and the hierarchial chip design would
>    be flattened and fractured and written to special control tapes in
>    the particular format (Perkin-Elmer etc) for the e-beam machine that
>    actually made the masks or reticles for each layer.  Now the chip
>    design can be encrypted and sent over the internet :)

Yes, this was done, but ruby-lith was used when the largest portion of 
the mask was to be opaque as opposed to tape which was used when it
was to be largely transparent. Tape outs were usually a combination of
the two. Depending on whether the mask was positive or negative, power 
metalization layers might be ruby while most of the chip was tape.

Of course, if the mask was a negative, this was all reversed.

One of the most unpleasant jobs I ever had was maintaining a Fortran
code to generate GDS files from a company's proprietary CAD data. It
was written in RatFor, but the company (who was dropping support for
th product) would only let us have the Fortran output of the
pre-processor. Every third line was a CONTINUE or a GOTO. There were
often several in a row:
200   CONTINUE
201   CONTINUE
202   CONTINUE

This all pre-dated the use of e-beam technology. The place was
installing its first e-beam system about the time I moved on. It was
not the most fun I ever had, but I learned a lot and, being fresh from
school, I really didn't know how to do anything practical, yet.
-- 
