I think you're really reaching with this theory.  First, you have to assume
that at least some people in Compaq have some common sense.  They did get
big enough to buy Digital.  Regardless of how they did this, the fact remains
that they DID do it.

So now you have this product that is responsible for in round numbers $4 billion
in annual revenues.  Further, this is high margin revenue.  Even if you are 99%
sure you could convert the revenue to another of your products, or that VMS will
eventually die regardless, there's still that 1% of doubt.  When you're ready
to put $4 billion (I don't know if I can imagine that much money) in annual
revenue at risk, I'd doubt that being 100% sure of your actions would be enough.
The small (very small) part of the VMS based revenue that Compaq spends on VMS
ongoing development is what I'd call a no-brainer type of decision.

As for Alpha, why would they want to kill it?  Not doing the FAB work, that
appeared to be a loser for DEC, is a problem that has gone away.  What Compaq
has now is a better handle on their future than any computer manufacturer that
is 100% dependant on Intel.  Before the 386 chip, buyers demanded second source
capability for chips.  Once Intel decided they had a good enough hold on the
customers, they stopped this practice.  If not for AMD and a few others, Intel
chip prices would be vastly different.  Is that the kind of sole source vendor
that you want to be 100% dependant upon?  Even if they're not interested in
doing the research for future Alpha chips themselves, (and from the looks of
things that's not the case), I'm sure Samsung, IBM, Intel, etc would take that
part over, gladly I'd guess.  Of course, if it was Intel, that's a black hole
I'd not want to be sucked into.

As a final note on trusting Intel, and what they say, if you'd bet your house
on their original release dates for Merced, you'd be on the street homeless
today.

I'm guessing you're still a bit ticked off about the cancellation of NT on
Alpha, and that's where these wild ideas are coming from.  Still, the passing
of time always brings surprises, and if you're right on this subject regardless
of all the reasons it sounds unlikely, I'll be one of you better customers for
which numbers to use in playing the lotto.

Dave

