The initial "memo". Compaq drops 32-bit Windows on Alpha. Says it will
continue Win64 development (the August 20 memo, courtesy of Acersoft and
SKC):

	http://www.acersoft.com/SKD/99/Strategy-Shift.htm

After the initial announcement, article on Microsoft's web site state
support for Win32 AND Win64 are being dropped (although, it reads more
that it was due to Compaq's initial decision):

	http://www.microsoft.com/ntserver/nts/news/msnw/compaq.asp

Here's something odd. The Microsoft article has the tags "Posted: August
24, 1999" and "Last Updated: Friday, August 27, 1999". Is it just me, or
didn't the article originally have a comparison between NT and T64
Unix?? The Matrix must have changed something... :)

Anyway, the two are dated several days apart (a weekend and a day to be
exact). I'm probably missing a few articles in between, but based on
these two articles it "appears" Compaq said "no 32-bit NT on Alpha", and
then Microsoft dropped the bomb and said "no-NT on Alpha period".

Although, looking back, there could be another scenario. Try this on for
size:

	Compaq is trying to gear up for Win64 on Alpha since they
	already have a 64-bit chip ready to run. In a badly-worded 
	memo, they state that they are only going to continue 
	support of 32-bit NT on Alpha up until SP6. It was meant to
	simply give customer sites a heads up. Microsoft reads this 
	memo the wrong way (upside down?) and decides to drop all
	support of WindowsNT/64 on Alpha altogether in a hasty 
	revenge. Compaq decides that if this is the way MS wants
	to do business, then so be it. Due to a misunderstanding,
	WinNT on Alpha vanishes.

This is only based on personal observation, opinion, and speculation.
Some fact to disprove me would be nice. 

Oooohhh... With speculation like this, perhaps I should become a trade
rag article writer... :)
