pasmith@ppg.com wrote in message <7r3b9h$ldn$1@nnrp1.deja.com>...
>We have an alpha 2100 that uses LAT to communicate with several
>terminal servers.  The dumb terminals on the terminal servers are
>predefined with LTA numbers to the 2100.  Applications which are
>running detached and triggered by the system startup file, communicate
>with these dumb terminals using the SYS$ASSIGN to define logical unit
>numbers.
>We are considering converting some of our terminal servers to run TCP
>protocol.  How do I predefine terminals on a TCP server so that the
>applications can still initiate communication with that device.
>We are running VMS 7.1 and UCX 4.1.
>Any assistance will be appreciated.
>

See the HELP TELNET /CREATE_SESSION.

Side effect of your conversion to be aware of:

Terminal server using LAT will signal a Data Set Hangup to your application
if a network problem occurs.  Application can easily detect and recover, or
at least die and let you know what happened.


Terminal Server using a TELNET port will not.  Application will hang if in
input wait.  You will have to find some way to detect and then terminate the
process.  TELNET port will need to be deleted  and recreated before it will
work again.  Have not tested to see if this is improved in a version of
Digital TCP/IP beyond 4.1.

Other than that little problem, as long as you are not using any of the LAT
specific QIO modifiers, everything will work fine.

I would only use this technique where LAT is absolutely not an option.

-John
.





