"Richard D. Piccard" <piccard@ohio.edu> writes:

> When integrated circuits are designed, the data file(s) that contain
> the information are (or were) often written to tape for transfer to
> the fabrication facility.  Hence, "taped out" means that the design
> phase (of at least this cycle of design-test-fix) is finished.

I think you are too young, Richard, though the concept is spot-on.

"Taping out" refers to the very old days when the actual photo mask
for the chip was created manually by placing opaque black tape on a
transparent film and then photo-reducing it to "real" size.

This is how things were done until about 25 (30?) years ago when photo
plotters were introduced by Gerber and some other company whose name
escapes me. (A senior moment, no doubt.)

Now days "taping out" does mean when the physical layout of the chip
is completed and the data is sent to the photo plotter for generation
of the masks to produce test chips. It's a big step in getting a chip
ready to go because it's the point you have to back up to to make ANY
changes.

This is made more complex on large chips where separate groups do
different parts of the chip and everything needs to be brought
together as the last step before prototyping.
-- 
