From: bdc@world.std.com (Brian Chase)
Subject: ANNOUNCE: NetBSD/vax 1.4.1 Released
Date: 1999/09/04
Message-ID: <FHK1Aw.FzK@world.std.com>
X-Deja-AN: 521126449
Organization: HappyNet Bungalow
Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.dec.micro,comp.org.decus,comp.unix.advocacy,alt.folklore.computers

ANNOUNCING NetBSD/vax 1.4.1

This is an informal announcement of the latest release of NetBSD/vax,
version 1.4.1.  The VAX architecture has been supported for some time now
under NetBSD, since the 1.1 days, but the 1.4.1 release represents sort of
a milestone.  Working SCSI support is now included for the VAXstation
2000, the VAXstation 3100s, and the oldest MicroVAX 3100s.  Additionally,
the installation tools on the VAX have been enhanced to allow for tape,
floppy, and network installs with the NetBSD sysinst facility.

Overall, the distribution is still a bit rough around the edges, but the
stability and functionality of 1.4.1 is quite high.  The SCSI support for
the VAXstation 2000, 3100s, and the MicroVAX 3100s is PIO based so it's
quite slow.  The SCSI transfer rates range from around 30-90KB/s depending
on the speed of the system in question.  Yes, it really is that slow. Disk
performance is much better on systems supporting MSCP and TMSCP devices
like the MicroVAX II.

The VAXstation 2000 and VAXstation 3100s support a graphic console but
there still isn't a functional Xserver yet for the platforms.  Other
systems like the MicroVAX II and the MicroVAX 3100s will require serial
consoles. And of course if you're running a VAX 11/780, you're going to
need a real console.

---

REQUIREMENTS - SYSTEMS SUPPORTED

  MASSBUS/BI/CMI/SBI/Unibus Systems
  ----------------------
  VAX 11/780 and 11/750
  VAX 8600 and 8650
  VAX 8200, 8250, 8300, and 8350

  Q-bus Systems
  -------------
  MicroVAX II and VAXstation II
  MicroVAX 3300 and 3400
  MicroVAX 3500 and 3600
  MicroVAX 3800 and 3900
  VAXstation 3200 and 3500

  Busless (sort of) Systems
  -------------------------
  VAXstation 2000 and MicroVAX 2000
  VAXstation 3100/M30 and 3100/M40
  VAXstation 3100/M38 and 3100/M48
  VAXstation 3100/M76
  MicroVAX 3100/10 and 3100/20
  MicroVAX 3100/10e and 3100/20e
  VAXserver 3100
  VT1300 (Just a diskless VAXstation 3100)

On the older MASSBUS and Unibus systems, most of the support tends to be
for the Unibus modules such as the MUX modules and the DELUA and DEUNA
ethernet modules.  Support for MSCP and TMSCP devices is included.

On the Q-bus systems there's support for the MSCP and TMSCP devices, a
variety of MUX modules and their clones, and the DEQNA and DELQA ethernet
modules.

The supported 2000 and 3100 systems have support for their LANCE ethernet,
the 5380 based SCSI controllers, their serial lines, and when present
their built-in monochrome frame buffers.  There's no MFM controller
support currently.  Contrary to peoples experiences with the VAXstation
and MicroVAX 2000 under VAX/VMS and Ultrix, with NetBSD you can use SCSI
disk drives on the built-in SCSI interface.  You can't bootstrap from
this interface as the standard ROM routines don't allow this.

REQUIREMENTS - GENERAL

NetBSD/vax can be reasonably run in around 4Megs of RAM, but I've done it
in as little as 3Megs.  With an appropriately stripped down kernel, you
may be able to push it even further than that.  For installing NetBSD/vax
using the sysinst images, the installation tools have been verified to work
with 6Megs of RAM.  They've also been verified to fail in 4Megs of RAM...
so 5Meg systems may or may not work.  (Actually, with enough
chicken-waving it *can* be done in 4Megs, but we leave that as an
exercise for the reader.)

You'll want to have around 200Megs of drive space to perform a full
install the system including the X clients.  Without the X clients, you
can just squeeze an install into 100Megs of space.  I've managed
installing onto a 100Meg Zip drive connected to one of my VAXstation
3100s.  If you're dealing with smaller drives, disks less than 300Megs, I
suggest that you create a single system partition in combination with a
single swap partition.  You'll have to choose follow the customized
install in sysinst to do this.

---

For the NetBSD/vax 1.4.1 distribution and installation notes, see:
  ftp://ftp.netbsd.org/pub/NetBSD/NetBSD-1.4.1/vax/

For the NetBSD/vax 1.4.1 precompiled binary packages, see:
  ftp://ftp.netbsd.org/pub/NetBSD/packages/1.4.1/vax/

Package descriptions can be found at the following location:
  ftp://ftp.netbsd.org/pub/NetBSD/packages/pkgsrc/README.html

For further details on the status and progress of NetBSD/vax, see:
  http://www.netbsd.org/Ports/vax/index.html

In order to subscribe to the NetBSD port-vax mailing list or peruse 
the mailing list archives, see:
  http://www.netbsd.org/MailingLists/index.html#port-vax

Work is currently underway for the VAXstation 4000 and VAX 4000 series,
several of which can operate in diskless netbooted configurations. Other
hot items include getting DMA based SCSI support functional on the
VAXstation and MicroVAX 3100s, and getting the VCB01 and VCB02 Q-bus
graphics modules operational.  

Special thanks go to Anders "Ragge" Magnusson, the NetBSD/vax portmaster
and David Brownlee who slogged his way through the buggy installation
tools and bootloader.  I also thank the NetBSD/vax community in general
for all their help in making this release as solid as it is.

-brian.
-- 
---
Brian "JARAI" Chase | http://world.std.com/~bdc/ | VAXZilla LIVES!!!



