The chip in question is not really that new - it's the 21364 which has
been discussed several times on this newsgroup. The article you
mention fleshes out some details not mentioned before, but may have
some mistakes (The Register is one of my favourite reads on the web
but it does resemble "The Sun" (UK tabloid) in many ways). For example
the lockstepping it mentions has been mentioned more often in
association with the 21464 as '364 is supposedly a '264 core on a
larger die which also contains a memory controller, 4 fast comms
links, an I/O controller, lots of cache etc. I don't think it will be
available this year or next, but I'd love to be proven wrong. It's
'464 which is the next wholly new core featuring hardware
multithreading etc.

If you search comp.arch archives (e.g. www.deja.com) for postings by
Burkhard Neidecker Lutz or Aaron Spink you may find the most accurate
info previously available. There is also info on the web about the
recent Microprocessor Forum which has some nice slides of '364 e.g. in
large multiprocessor configurations using the on-chip links.

Cheers,

Chris
