hassani youssouf&toko suzuki wrote in message
<37D43BB3.1BA4111D@freenet.co.uk>...
>Hi I have been offered a vax server 3400+decserver 200/mc +dumb terminal
>
>where can I start ?

Try putting a title on your message that more closely describes what you are
looking for.  There are 50 to 100 messages posted a day on comp.os.vms and
comp.sys.dec, and many will go unread.

The OpenVMS home page: http://www.openvms.digital.com

Look for the links to the OpenVMS FAQ.  And the links to the OpenVMS Wizard.

Look for the links to the OpenVMS Hobbyist License that will take you to
http://www.montagar.com/hobbyist

If you are interested getting a free hobbyist license for your system, see
http://www.decus.org for information on joining.

The OpenVMS FAQ will provide you with some links to archives.  One that is
easily searched is http://www.deja.com.  For your hardware, "comp.os.vms"
"hoffman" "serpentine" are good keywords.

See also http://anacin.nsc.vcu.edu/~jim/mvax/mvax_faq_text.html and
http://montagar.com/hobbyist/links.html

What you have found is a rather old and slow VAX.  It is rated at about 2
VUPs.  The other box is a terminal server.  What it does is allow the
connection of 8 serial devices to your VAX if you have your VAX set up to
work with it.

The DECserver 200MC requires a host computer such a VAX to supply it with a
boot file when it powers up.  IIRC: This software is known as PRO801.SYS.

The VAX 3400 is a Q-BUS based VAX.  On a q-bus based VAX, the parts have two
names.  The first name is one such as KA-640-nn, where the nn is a variant
option.  This is the order part number name.  The second name is a module
number, that is printed or stamped somewhere on the part.  A particular part
may have several modules each with their own number.  Either number can
usually be used to find the part if you do a search or need to ask a
question.

The good news on the 3400, is that it has a built in eithernet port, and
DSSI disk adapter.

The bad news is that DSSI disks may be very expensive to add or replace.  Of
course you can get lucky and find a free one.  They are still making DSSI
disks, but they are not as good as a buy as SCSI disks.

Memory boards for the unit can also be either expensive or free, depending
on your sources.

If you are lucky, you may find a third-party SCSI disk controller on it, or
some other type of controller that will allow you to connect a CDROM.

With that age of computer equipment, the trick is to catch it before it goes
into the dumpster.

If you have an RF71 disk, and at least 14M of RAM, that is adequate for
getting started with Hobby VMS system.  You will also need to find someone
local to you that can help you copy the savesets from the hobbyist CDROM to
a TKnn tape that your system can handle.


If you do need to buy something for it, you may find a used VAXstation 4000
series on one of the online auctions as cheaper to purchase or add SCSI
disks and Memory.  Or even a used ALPHA that can run OpenVMS.

-John





