	According to EE Times , 21364 is scheduled to tapeout in 
	December:

http://www.eet.com/story/OEG19990903S0010

"The next-generation version, the EV-7 (or 21364), is scheduled to tape out in
December. It's intended to support clock speeds in excess of 1 GHz and will
include 1.5 Mbytes of integrated L2 cache, a 6-Gbyte/ second direct Rambus
memory controller, a 3-Gbyte/s I/O interface and a direct
processor-to-processor interface. The package is designed to support
large-scale multiprocessing and high-availability systems."

	The Register piece offers a few more titallating details.

http://www.theregister.co.uk/990906-000008.html

		o  1.6 GHz   
		o  128 bit cache filled in one transfer, contrast:
		o  4 transfers to fill 64 bit Merced cache

	I think that last tidbit is rather telling.  Perhaps Merced is
	avoiding Rambus like the plague because with its chipset it will
	incure punishing latency delays.  No doubt.

	I'm surprised 21364 hasn't reached tape out and won't tape out
	until December.  Would be a nice scoop if The Register could dig
	up just how close 21364 is to tape out.  "Readying" sounds as if
	it has already taped out.

