JF Mezei wrote:
[snip]
> What I meant is that it was wrong to call the Intel engineers incapable.

   I didn't say incapable, I said junior (i.e. inexperienced)

> They
> were given a very difficult task to make a single chip that has 2.5
> architectures supported inside, instead of focusing on a single high
> performance architecture.

   Until we learn more about it in October the quality of the merced
   design can only be judged in broad terms by comparison with the
   follow-on IA-64 chip called McKinley (which is largely the product
   of veteran HP engineers that formerly designed PA-RISC chips).
   Here are some comments directly or indirectly (MDR) attributed
   to Intel or HP:

   1)  merced and mckinley will be made in the same 0.18um process
   2)  the CPU core of mckinley is smaller than the core of merced
   3)  mckinley will operate at higher clock rates than merced
   4)  mckinley will have shorter pipelines than merced
   5)  mckinley will achieve better utilization of its functional
       units than merced.
   6)  mckinley will achieve at least twice the performance as
       merced (again, in the same process)

   Consider 3) and 4).  Frequency and pipeline depth are related
   design parameters. Usually more pipe stages you have the faster
   your CPU can clock.  The fact that Mckinley is both faster and
   has shorter pipelines indicates that merced is a significantly
   sub-optimal design.

   Consider 1), 2), and 6).  Given postulate 4), it is unlikely
   that mckinley is clocked 2X as fast as merced to achieve its 2X
   performance. That means that mckinley can accomplish more work
   per cycle than merced which usually implies more gates and CPU
   core area.  Yet mckinley has a *smaller* CPU core (although in
   all fairness it is possible that mckinley doesn't have its x86
   compatibility in silicon like merced; but this is a small part
   of the merced floorplan).  So this implies that merced wasn't
   nearly as well partitioned, floorplanned, and implemented as it
   could have been.

   Consider 5), and 6).  Usually a higher performance processor
   achieves that characteristic by having more functional units.
   But each extra functional unit is less often utilized than the
   last due to limits in the instruction level parallelism in
   application code. If mckinley is achieving both higher performance
   and higher FU utilization then this implies the merced is a rather
   constipated design with unnecessary restrictions to the issue,
   execution, and/or retirement of instructions.

   There is no such a thing as a free lunch.  The fact that Mckinley
   designers are apparently enjoying a couple of them implies that
   the merced, an exclusively Intel chip project, is far less than
   what it could have been.  But hey, but that is how junior engineers
   learn their craft and become better, more seasoned senior engineers.
   If you cannot do better on your next chip then you should retire or
   go into management or something :)

> 
> I wonder if in hindsight, Intel and HP regret forcing those engineers to
> integrate the 8086 and other stuff. Since the Merced probably won't have much
> of a performance advantage over the version of the 8086 available at the time
> Merced starts to sell, it will be very interesting to see how they position
> the two chips.

   Silicon based x86 compatibility in IA-64 CPUs will likely die
   off soon, maybe even in Mckinley.


All opinions strictly my own.
