  David A Froble <davef@tsoft-inc.com> wrote:
> Hoff Hoffman wrote:

> I'm including an Idea I had when replying to another posting.
>
> I have the firm opinion that selection of applications is something
users
> do, not the hardware/OS vendor.

This is a rather simplistic view

1) Individual users do not have much clout

2) They have even less clout if the OS/Platform is not the most
   popular for the application.

3) They have even less clout if the OS vendor has been allowing
   the impression to grow that the OS is dying and that people
   should migrate to other more popular platforms.
   Any application vendor looking to cut costs will then say why
   support this platform if  everyone will be moving anyway.

4) Once an application is dropped (or if an application has never
   been supported) it is very unlikely many people will ask for
   it. It takes a special kind of user to ask for a product which
   is not already shown as being available on an application
   vendors web site. Most just look to use one of the supported
   OS/platforms or look for another product.
   Indeed I have come across application vendors in the past whose
   web sites do not mention VMS but do actually have a VMS version of
   their product. They then seem suprised that they dont get more
   new VMS customers which can lead to them then dropping the
   VMS version.




>However, I could see Compaq helping
in this
> effort by collecting the names of customers interested in a product
and
> taking this data to the software vendor.  They might not get excited
each
> time a single customer approaches them, but let Compaq come to them
with
> a list of 10 or 100 serious prospects, and most will take a closer
look
> at the issue.
>

Compaq should also be talking directly to these software vendors to
correct the "VMS is dying image" of the past. To try and make
sure that software vendors advertise the fact that VMS versions
exist. To provide assistance in porting applications to VMS.
Indeed sometime ago I was told that Digital/Compaq had a fund
of seed money to help get applications onto VMS. I haven't heard
much since.

Sybase in particular seems an odd case. Digital/Compaq have been
promoting VMS as the high end database system and then let
one of the major databases drop VMS without a murmur.


David Webb
VMS and Unix team leader
CCSS
Middlesex University


