Alan seems to have interpreted your last sentence as referring to the
Jupiter cancellation.  I think you were referring to the recent NT on Alpha
cancellation.

If so, IMO that's pure BS - and this opinion is from someone who already
thought many people here had their heads in the sand w.r.t. long-term Compaq
disinterest in supporting Alpha and its captive OSs.  But, unless you
discount completely Compaq's public stance up to the last couple of weeks
(and although I'm beginning to think that trusting *any* Compaq public
statement may be grounds for a competency hearing, this too is only a very
*recent* occurrence), expecting a significant short-term move *against*
Alpha seemed contrary to Compaq's short-term economic interest (which,
unlike their statements, might be expected to carry some weight).

I do take some exception to Dave's feeling that the Jupiter situation was
entirely different, since publicly (and internally), right up until Jupiter
was cancelled, DEC was also saying that the 10/20 architecture had a future
coexistence with VAX far beyond mere maintenance (though, again, it was
obvious that some internal tension existed between the two architectures).
The internal story at the time was that early Jupiter hardware failed to
live up to the (perhaps excessively optimistic, depending on whom one
believes) performance claimed for it, but that doesn't change the fact that
DEC was pushing the architecture right up to the point where it cancelled
Jupiter (and the architecture's future along with it, though I can't recall
the degree to which 36-bit software development continued for a while
thereafter - so that may be what Dave was contrasting).

- bill





